<PubmedArticleSet><PubmedArticle><MedlineCitation Status="MEDLINE" Owner="NLM" IndexingMethod="Automated"><PMID Version="1">37193694</PMID><DateCompleted><Year>2023</Year><Month>05</Month><Day>18</Day></DateCompleted><DateRevised><Year>2024</Year><Month>06</Month><Day>01</Day></DateRevised><Article PubModel="Electronic"><Journal><ISSN IssnType="Electronic">2041-1723</ISSN><JournalIssue CitedMedium="Internet"><Volume>14</Volume><Issue>1</Issue><PubDate><Year>2023</Year><Month>May</Month><Day>16</Day></PubDate></JournalIssue><Title>Nature communications</Title><ISOAbbreviation>Nat Commun</ISOAbbreviation></Journal><ArticleTitle>Identification of indocyanine green as a STT3B inhibitor against mushroom &#945;-amanitin cytotoxicity.</ArticleTitle><Pagination><StartPage>2241</StartPage><MedlinePgn>2241</MedlinePgn></Pagination><ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">2241</ELocationID><ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/s41467-023-37714-3</ELocationID><Abstract><AbstractText>The "death cap", Amanita phalloides, is the world's most poisonous mushroom, responsible for 90% of mushroom-related fatalities. The most fatal component of the death cap is &#945;-amanitin. Despite its lethal effect, the exact mechanisms of how &#945;-amanitin poisons humans remain unclear, leading to no specific antidote available for treatment. Here we show that STT3B is required for &#945;-amanitin toxicity and its inhibitor, indocyanine green (ICG), can be used as a specific antidote. By combining a genome-wide CRISPR screen with an in silico drug screening and in vivo functional validation, we discover that N-glycan biosynthesis pathway and its key component, STT3B, play a crucial role in &#945;-amanitin toxicity and that ICG is a STT3B inhibitor. Furthermore, we demonstrate that ICG is effective in blocking the toxic effect of &#945;-amanitin in cells, liver organoids, and male mice, resulting in an overall increase in animal survival. Together, by combining a genome-wide CRISPR screen for &#945;-amanitin toxicity with an in silico drug screen and functional validation in vivo, our study highlights ICG as a STT3B inhibitor against the mushroom toxin.</AbstractText><CopyrightInformation>&#169; 2023. The Author(s).</CopyrightInformation></Abstract><AuthorList CompleteYN="Y"><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Wang</LastName><ForeName>Bei</ForeName><Initials>B</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0009-0002-7992-6414</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Metabolism and Aging, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Shenzhen), Shenzhen Campus of Sun Yat-sen University, Shenzhen, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Wan</LastName><ForeName>Arabella H</ForeName><Initials>AH</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Department of Pathology, The First Affiliated Hospital, Sun Yat-sen University, Guangzhou, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y" EqualContrib="Y"><LastName>Xu</LastName><ForeName>Yu</ForeName><Initials>Y</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Metabolism and Aging, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Shenzhen), Shenzhen Campus of Sun Yat-sen University, Shenzhen, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhang</LastName><ForeName>Ruo-Xin</ForeName><Initials>RX</Initials><Identifier Source="ORCID">0000-0002-8252-3141</Identifier><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Metabolism and Aging, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Shenzhen), Shenzhen Campus of Sun Yat-sen University, Shenzhen, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</LastName><ForeName>Ben-Chi</ForeName><Initials>BC</Initials><AffiliationInfo><Affiliation>Laboratory of Metabolism and Aging, School of Pharmaceutical Sciences (Shenzhen), Shenzhen Campus of Sun Yat-sen University, Shenzhen, PR China.</Affiliation></AffiliationInfo></Author><Author ValidYN="Y"><LastName>Zhao</L